---
// Goal: show that a ThinkThen question works as a function over a list,
// a column or a stream, and that the whole list goes to one call.
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Terminal from '../../components/Terminal.astro';
import Code from '../../components/Code.astro';
import { FUNCTIONAL_PIPE_SEE } from '../../data/catalog.mjs';
import { installSample, run } from '../../data/samples.mjs';
import { REPO } from '../../data/repo.mjs';

const judge = installSample('python', 'functional/judge');
const stream = installSample('python', 'functional/stream');
const tally = installSample('python', 'functional/tally');
const pipe = { ...run('functions/filter', '1-lines'), see: FUNCTIONAL_PIPE_SEE };
const readme = (folder) => `${REPO}/blob/main/libraries/${folder}/README.md`;
---
<Base title="Functional patterns" description="Bind a ThinkThen question once, then apply it to a list, a column or a stream. The whole list packs into as few requests as the batch settings allow.">
  <h1>Functional patterns</h1>
  <p class="lede">A ThinkThen question works like a function. Bind the question once. Then apply it to text, a list, a column or a stream.</p>

  <h2>Reuse one question</h2>
  <p>Development 0.2 uses named methods on one Python <code>Engine</code>. Keep the question as text or an authored dictionary, then pass it with the input to each call. A whole list allows Rust to pack requests; calling once per item creates separate calls.</p>
  <p><code>engine.plan</code> previews a complete input without sending. Its request admission bound precedes cache reuse and packing. Iterators cannot be previewed.</p>
  <Code code={judge.code} file={judge.file} label="Copy the judge sample" />
  {judge.output && <Code code={judge.output} lang="output" caption="What Python printed" copy={false} />}

  <h2>A stream stops when you stop</h2>
  <p>Pass a generator to <code>engine.iterate</code> to obtain a lazy typed stream. The stream reads ahead a little and packs what it read. When the reader stops, ThinkThen stops sending. Here the reader takes the first complaint and closes the stream.</p>
  <Code code={stream.code} file={stream.file} label="Copy the stream sample" />
  {stream.output && <Code code={stream.output} lang="output" caption="What Python printed" copy={false} />}
  <p>A slow source can leave a request half full while it waits. Set <code>batch=1</code> on the engine for a live source.</p>

  <h2>Count every call</h2>
  <p>Each completed call retains its own facts. This engine reads four reviews, then two. Add those settled counts when your caller needs a total.</p>
  <Code code={tally.code} file={tally.file} label="Copy the tally sample" />
  {tally.output && <Code code={tally.output} lang="output" caption="What Python printed" copy={false} />}

  <h2>R</h2>
  <p>Development 0.2 uses <code>tt_decide(question, input)</code> with both arguments. Read its generated results and facts. The <a href={readme('r')}>R guide</a> maps removed judge closures and simplified frames.</p>

  <h2>The shell</h2>
  <p>The pipe is the same idea. <code>filter</code> reads a stream of lines and keeps the ones whose answer is yes.</p>
  <Terminal run={pipe} caption={pipe.see} />

  <h2>More</h2>
  <p>The <a href={readme('python')}>Python README</a> covers named methods over Polars and pandas columns, deadlines and cancel tokens. The <a href={readme('r')}>R README</a> covers dbplyr. The <a href={readme('polars')}>Rust Polars README</a> covers lazy expressions. <a href="/learn/caching/">Caching and replay</a> shows why a second run sends nothing.</p>

  <p class="pagelinks"><a href="/install/python/">Python</a> · <a href="/install/r/">R</a> · <a href="/functions/filter/">filter</a></p>
</Base>
